In the second Figure the middle Term is the Predicate of both the Premises; this contains four Moods (viz.) Cefare, Cameftres, Festino, Baroco, and it admits only of negative Conclusions; as,
Ce- No Liar is fit to be believed;
fa- Every good Christian is fit to be believed;
re. Therefore no good Christian is a Liar.
The Reader may easily form Examples of the rest.
The third Figure requires that the middle Term be the Subject of both the Premisses. It has six Moods (viz.) Darapti, Felapton, Disamis, Datifi, Bocardo, Ferison: And it admits only of particular Conclusions; as,
